WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:11.320
Mathematical-based Python libraries like NumPy and like Pandas.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:18.869
These are libraries that can help spot trends over time, gain insights into data,

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:25.570
and maybe one day, even solve the mystery of just why seven eight nine.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:30.640
So today, we're going to take a closer look at NumPy and Pandas.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.840
And if you've ever seen a simple ray of sunlight plus a glass prism,

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:42.579
you've seen how that combination lets us see all the colors of the visible spectrum hidden inside.

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:46.660
Well, when a data scientist comes across some interesting new data

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:51.280
and they want to get a deeper look, they've got a number of tools they reach for.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:57.940
Now, this would be a great time for some background music, but I'm... I'm being told that that's not in the budget.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:06.924
Now Python, P-Y-T-H-O-N --Python is probably the language most associated with data science,

00:01:06.924 --> 00:01:13.210
but it's not really Python itself providing these deep perspective shifting capabilities.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:20.230
It's usually some sort of Python library which specializes in numerical and data processing.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:23.920
And two of the biggest ones out there are, oh yes!

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.649
NumPy and Pandas.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.909
So which one is the right one for us?

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.180
Is there a clear winner in this mathematical match up?

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:42.743
Well, for starters, we're not in for too intense of a brawl here since Pandas is actually built on top of NumPy.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:48.895
So even if we're fully Team Pandas, we're still using NumPy.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:02:00.970
Now, NumPy was released as an open source project back in 2005 with the goal of bringing scientific computing to Python.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.129
It was based on two earlier packages.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:10.684
Those packages were Numeric and the other package was Numarray.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:16.809
And its strength really lies in its ability to work with multi-dimensional array objects.

00:02:17.513 --> 00:02:23.343
From there, users can sort search, filter, apply linear algebra, Fourier transforms--

00:02:23.343 --> 00:02:30.789
the tools the data scientist needs to handle large amounts of data much faster than they could with Python's built-in functions.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:39.710
Specifically, it leverages something called BLAS-- that is an acronym for Basic Linear Algebra Subprogram,

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:50.680
and LAPACK, which is also an acronym, Linear Algebra PACKage. And it uses those to supercharge its linear algebra capabilities.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:54.490
So all good, why not just stop there?

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:56.371
Why not stay comfortably NumPy?

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:01.179
Well, as its name suggests, NumPy is all about numbers.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.939
And where it really excels is numerical analysis, linear algebra and simulations.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:10.075
But when it comes to data analysis of manipulation,

00:03:10.075 --> 00:03:17.630
working with a wide range of data sources, that's where Pandas really starts to differentiate itself.

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:25.045
Now, Pandas got its start in 2008 when developer Wes McKinney was looking for a powerful

00:03:25.045 --> 00:03:29.349
and flexible tool for programing quantitative analysis on financial data.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:39.970
Now Pandas is named after the three dimensional PANel DAta of which it works in.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:43.240
And then it was made open source the following year.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:48.686
Now, Pandas makes the process of working with data more straightforward for data scientists

00:03:48.686 --> 00:03:54.340
by providing methods for loading, reshaping, pivoting, merging and joining data.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:56.560
Or even working with missing data.

00:03:57.010 --> 00:04:07.539
It excels at working with tabular data, whereas NumPy is really more firmly rooted strictly in numerical data.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:20.217
Where NumPy excels at things like simulation, well, Pandas steps up its game in things like data analysis.

00:04:21.089 --> 00:04:23.999
So why not start right with Pandas?

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:32.639
After all, most of NumPy's methods get surfaced outward through Pandas, so one might see this as a superset.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:39.273
Well, Pandas does build on top of NumPy, but that also means that it brings with it some overhead,

00:04:39.273 --> 00:04:42.660
both in terms of performance and learning curve.

00:04:43.165 --> 00:04:46.829
Pandas capabilities come at a cost of complexity.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:53.782
However, Pandas also implements a number of functions optimized with C and Cython,

00:04:53.782 --> 00:05:00.424
which can be faster than the NumPy equivalent once we get into very large datasets.

00:05:00.749 --> 00:05:08.190
The general consensus on the best approach seems to be start with NumPy and look for the features you're most likely to need.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:12.450
If that search leads you to Pandas, then there's your answer.

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:21.300
So if you came here looking for a knockdown, drag out fight between Pandas and NumPy, I hope you're not too disappointed.

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:27.360
That landscape of mathematical and scientific tools available to us keeps us busy and well equipped.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:34.769
So when you're thinking Pandas, NumPy or anything else, it's really any color you like.

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:38.500
If you have any questions, please drop us a line below.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:43.120
And if you want to see more videos like this in the future, please like and subscribe.

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:44.690
Thanks for watching.

